# 3. Smart menu experience

## 3.1 Menu entry points

Customers can reach the menu through:

- **Browse food**;
- restaurant link;
- QR code;
- campaign CTA;
- reorder flow;
- “show me the menu”;
- a natural-language request.

## 3.2 Restaurant discovery card

Show only restaurants that are:

- open or accepting scheduled orders;
- inside the delivery area;
- able to fulfil the requested time;
- not paused;
- relevant to the request.

Each restaurant card should contain:

```text
Restaurant name
Cuisine/category
Approximate preparation time
Delivery fee
Minimum order if relevant
Rating or quality signal when reliable
Open/closed state
```

Do not overload the customer with ten restaurants. Start with three strong results and offer **More restaurants**.

## 3.3 Category menu

Use a WhatsApp list for categories:

```text
Popular
Shawarma
Pizza
Burgers
Family meals
Vegetarian
Desserts
Drinks
```

WhatsApp list constraints must be enforced server-side:

- maximum 10 rows;
- short row titles;
- concise descriptions;
- pagination for more categories.

## 3.4 Item cards

An item card should include:

```text
Photo, when available
Item name
Short description
Verified allergen information, only if kitchen-reviewed
Price in USD and LBP
Availability
Add
Choose options
Quantity
Back to menu
```

Use buttons such as:

```text
Add $6.00
Choose options
Menu
```

Prices are shown from a server-built response only.

## 3.5 Smart recommendations

When the customer asks “what’s good,” rank using:

1. hard dietary/allergen constraints;
2. current request relevance;
3. delivery serviceability;
4. restaurant open state;
5. explicit preferences;
6. successful order history;
7. time of day;
8. basket compatibility;
9. reliability and preparation time;
10. controlled new-restaurant exploration.

Paid campaigns must never silently change organic ranking.

## 3.6 Recommendation language

Good:

> Since you usually choose shawarma, this family platter from an open restaurant nearby could work well. It serves four and stays within your usual budget.

Avoid:

> We know you order shawarma every Friday and spend $18–$25.

The system may store detailed reasons internally, but customer-facing explanations should be natural and non-invasive.

