# 8. Notifications strategy

## 8.1 Notification principles

- Notify only when the information is useful.
- One notification per meaningful status change.
- Use the customer’s language.
- Use the customer service window when open.
- Use approved utility templates when a message must be sent outside the window.
- Never claim delivery without a verified event.
- Deduplicate every notification by order and status.

## 8.2 Customer notification table

| Event | Customer message | CTA |
|---|---|---|
| Order created | Order received with code and summary | Cancel while pending |
| Accepted | Restaurant accepted; estimated time | Track/order help |
| Preparing | Usually no message; status stored | None |
| Delayed | New ETA and short explanation | Contact support |
| Ready | Order ready for pickup or dispatch | Track |
| Out for delivery | Driver/tracking link and ETA | Track driver |
| Delivered | Delivered successfully | Rate order |
| Rejected | Apology and reason if safe | Choose another restaurant |
| Cancelled | Cancellation confirmed | Reorder/menu |
| Substitution needed | Item issue and alternatives | Swap/remove/cancel |
| Payment issue | Exact next step | Retry/contact support |

## 8.3 Notification templates

### Accepted

> ✅ **[Restaurant]** accepted order **#[code]**. Estimated preparation: **[minutes] min**. We’ll let you know when it’s on the way.

### Out for delivery

> 🛵 Your order **#[code]** is on the way. Estimated arrival: **[time]**. Track it here: **[link]**

### Delivered

> 🎉 Order **#[code]** was delivered. Enjoy your meal! How was it?

Buttons:

```text
1 ⭐
2 ⭐
3 ⭐
4 ⭐
5 ⭐
```

### Delay

> We’re sorry — **[Restaurant]** needs about **[new ETA]** more minutes. Do you want to keep waiting or speak with our team?

## 8.4 Notification failure policy

If sending fails:

1. record the failure code;
2. retry according to a bounded schedule;
3. do not send duplicate customer messages;
4. use a template if the service window is closed and the message is eligible;
5. show the status in the dashboard;
6. alert operations for high-impact failures.

